11. Handle Dynamic Parameters Using Regular Expression Extractor
In performance testing, dynamic parameters such as session IDs, tokens, or request IDs often change with each test run. If not handled properly, these parameters can cause test failures or incorrect test results. JMeter’s Regular Expression Extractor allows you to capture these values and reuse them in subsequent requests.
11.1 Identify Dynamic Parameters
Before extracting:
Record a full test flow using HTTP(S) Test Script Recorder.
Compare successive test runs to spot values that change dynamically (e.g., session_id, authToken, viewState, etc.).
Look in Request/Response bodies or headers where the value is generated or returned.
11.2 Add Regular Expression Extractor
To extract a dynamic value:
Select the request (sampler) that receives the response containing the dynamic value.
Right-click on the request → Add → Post Processors → Regular Expression Extractor.
Example Setup:
Setting	Value
Name	Extract Session ID
Apply to	Main sample only
Field to check	Body (or Header, URL, etc., based on where the value appears)
Reference Name	session_id
Regular Expression	session_id":"(\w+)"
Template	$1$
Match No.	1
Default Value	NOT_FOUND
✅ This will store the extracted value in a variable ${session_id}.
11.3 Use the Extracted Variable
Now, use the ${session_id} in any subsequent request that needs this token.
Example:
In the next HTTP Request:
Authorization: Bearer ${session_id}
Or in the request body:
{"sessionId": "${session_id}"}
11.4 Tips & Best Practices
Always give meaningful names to extractors (e.g., Extract_CSRFToken, GetOrderID).
Use Debug Sampler and View Results Tree to confirm your value was captured correctly.
If the parameter is missing, check for:
Incorrect regular expression
Wrong "Field to check" option
Extraction from the wrong sampler
Set a default value to avoid null variables crashing your test (e.g., NOT_FOUND).






12. Group Requests Using Transaction Controller
In real-world scenarios, a single business action (like logging in, searching, or checking out) involves multiple HTTP requests. Grouping these related requests into one Transaction Controller helps simulate the full action as a single unit and measure its aggregate performance (overall time for all included steps).
12.1 Purpose of Transaction Controller
Combines multiple samplers (requests) into a logical transaction block.
Measures overall response time for the entire action, not just individual requests.
Provides cleaner reports by showing the duration of complete business actions.
Makes test results easier to read, especially for performance analysis.
12.2 How to Add a Transaction Controller
Right-click on your Thread Group → Add → Logic Controller → Transaction Controller.
Give it a descriptive name (e.g., Login_Transaction, Search_Transaction).
Drag and drop all relevant HTTP Requests under this controller.
(Optional but recommended) Check the box:
✅ Generate parent sample
This allows JMeter to report a single entry for the whole transaction in results.
12.3 Example Use Case
Login Scenario:
GET /loginPage.jsp
POST /authenticateUser
GET /homePage.jsp
All of the above steps can be grouped under a Transaction Controller named Login_Transaction.
12.4 Reporting Behavior
If “Generate parent sample” is checked, you will see only the transaction in the results (with total time).
If unchecked, JMeter shows the transaction and each individual request separately.
This is useful if you want both detailed and grouped stats.
12.5 Best Practices
Use one Transaction Controller per business action.
Always name them clearly (e.g., Search_Transaction, Checkout_Flow).
Always verify that the requests are placed inside the controller, not alongside.
Consider nesting multiple transactions under Simple Controllers for structure.







13. Add Think Time and Timers
In real-world scenarios, users don’t click through a website instantly. They pause to read, fill out forms, or review content. This delay is referred to as think time. In JMeter, Timers are used to simulate these pauses and create more realistic test execution patterns.
Without timers, JMeter threads (virtual users) execute requests in rapid succession, putting unnatural load on the server.
13.1 Types of Timers in JMeter
Here are the most commonly used timers to simulate think time and delay:
a. Constant Timer
Introduces a fixed delay (in milliseconds) before each request.
Example:
A Constant Timer of 3000 adds a 3-second pause before the sampler.
Use When:
You want consistent delay (e.g., waiting between login and dashboard load).
b. Gaussian Random Timer
Adds a random delay with a Gaussian (bell curve) distribution.
Parameters:
Constant Delay Offset – base delay in ms (e.g., 2000)
Deviation – range of variation (e.g., 500)
Example: Simulates an average 2s delay, with natural variation ±500ms.
Use When:
You want realistic delays to mimic varied user behavior.
c. Uniform Random Timer
Adds random delay within a fixed range.
Parameters:
Random Delay Maximum – max added time
Constant Delay Offset – base delay
Example: Delay = offset + random(0 to max)
Use When:
You want a flat range of variability (less natural than Gaussian).
13.2 How to Add a Timer
Right-click on the Sampler (or Controller) where you want to add delay.
Navigate to:
Add → Timer → [Choose Timer Type]
Configure the timer values based on the type (e.g., 3000 for 3 seconds).
📌 Timers apply before the sampler they're attached to.
13.3 Where to Place Timers
Placement	Effect
Before individual samplers	Adds delay before that request only
Under a Transaction Controller	Applies delay before each request within the transaction
Under a Thread Group	Adds delay before every request in the test (not usually ideal)
13.4 Best Practices
Simulate realistic behavior: Users typically pause 1–5 seconds between actions.
Use Gaussian Random Timer for natural-feeling delays.
Avoid using too many Constant Timers — it can cause artificial synchronization.
Don’t place timers at the Test Plan level — keep delays targeted.

